   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to directing the New\nYork city transit authority to include instructions on how to react when a\nperson falls onto the subway tracks in its ongoing safety education campaigns;\nand repealing certain provisions of such law relating to requiring the New\nYork city transit authority advisory council to investigate, report and make\nrecommendations on instructing the public on how to react when an individual\nfalls onto authority facility tracks",
    "basePrintNo": "S7822",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7822-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1204-f, rpld §1204-e sub 5, Pub Auth L (as proposed in S.3160-A & A.935-A)",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7822\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to directing the\nNew York city transit authority to include instructions on how to react\nwhen a person falls onto the subway tracks in its ongoing safety educa-\ntion campaigns; and repealing certain provisions of such law relating to\nrequiring the New York city transit authority advisory council to inves-\ntigate, report and make recommendations on instructing the public on how\nto react when an individual falls onto authority facility tracks\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend the Public Authorities Law to\nrequire the New York City Transit Authority to provide instructions on\nhow to react when a person falls on subway tracks.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: The bill amends the New York City Transit Authority's enact-\ning statute to put an affirmative requirement in place for the Authority\nto provide instructions on how to react in a safe, timely, and effective\nmanner when a person falls onto the subway tracks.\n\nSection 2: The bill modifies the previous chapter on this subject, which\nwould have required a study that would be redundant to what the Authori-\nty is already doing in conjunction with the Metropolitan Transportation\nAuthority (MTA).\n\nSection 3: Section 3 contains the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn average, 134 people fall onto the subway tracks and are struck by\ntrains each year, with 41 of those resulting in a fatality. In 2012, 141\npeople were struck, with 55 fatalities, and in 2013, 151 people were\nstruck with 53 fatalities. Despite these incidents, the MTA had no\nestablished public instruction on the best way to react in the event\nthat an individual falls onto the tracks. This legislation now requires\nthe Authority to incorporate such education into its ongoing safety\ncampaigns.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021: Underlying legislation (S.3160-A/A.935-A) passed both houses unan-\nimously and was signed by the Executive.\n2019-20: Essentially identical version (S.3228-A) passed Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNo negative impacts anticipated.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date as the underlying chapter,\nwhich was immediate.\n",
